          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>EXPLANATION</p>",
            "readable_text": "EXPLANATION",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x(y+1)]</span> if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: x\\mathbin{:}]</span></p><p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (x)/(x) \u22c5 (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y + xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y(1 + x))/(x(1 + y))]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: x(y+1)] if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: x\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (x)/(x) \u22c5 (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y + xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y(1 + x))/(x(1 + y))]",
            "images": [],
