    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen two fractions have the same denominator, we add the numerators. For example,\n[MATH: \\dfrac 1 7 + \\dfrac 5 7 = \\dfrac{1+5}{7} = \\dfrac 6 7.]\nIn this case, both fractions were over a common denominator of [MATH: 7].\n\nContent:\nIt works the same with rational expressions! So suppose that we want to add\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{x} + \\dfrac{5}{x}.]\n\nContent:\nBecause both fractions have the same denominator of [MATH: x,] we can add the numerators as before:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{1}{x} + \\dfrac{5}{x} = \\dfrac{1+5}{x} = \\dfrac{6}{x}]\n\nContent:\nLet's see some more examples!\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\dfrac{2+a}{2a} + \\dfrac{3+2a}{2a}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe have a common denominator of [MATH: 2a,] so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n[MATH: (2 + a)/(2a) + (3 + 2a)/(2a)|=; (2 + a + 3 + 2a)/(2a)|=; (2 + 3 + a + 2a)/(2a)|=; (5 + 3a)/(2a)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (5 + b)/(3b) + (6 \u2212 b)/(3b).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (11)/(3b)]\nb. [MATH: (2b + 1)/(3b)]\nc. [MATH: (11 + 2b)/(3b)]\nd. [MATH: (2b \u2212 1)/(3b)]\ne. [MATH: (11 \u2212 2b)/(3b)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (3y \u2212 z)/(5z) \u2212 (4y \u2212 3z)/(5z).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2z \u2212 y)/(5z)]\nb. [MATH: (z \u2212 y)/(5z)]\nc. [MATH: (2z + y)/(z)]\nd. [MATH: (4z \u2212 y)/(z)]\ne. [MATH: (7y \u2212 4z)/(2z)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Tutorial: Adding Rational Expressions Without Common Denominators\n\nContent:\nRemember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like [MATH: \\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8,] we need to put the fractions over a common denominator, as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (1)/(4) + (1)/(8)|=; (2)/(8) + (1)/(8)|=; (3)/(8)]\n\nContent:\nIt works the same in algebraic rational expressions! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators.\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators\n\nExample Prompt:\nGiven that [MATH: \\dfrac 3 {2x}=a] and [MATH: \\dfrac y x=b,] what is [MATH: a+b?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe wish to add the following fractions:\n[MATH: a+b = \\dfrac 3 {2x} + \\dfrac y x]\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: 2x] if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: 2\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n\\dfrac 3 {2x} + \\dfrac y x &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac 3 {2x} + \\dfrac 2 2 \\cdot \\dfrac y x &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac 3 {2x} + \\dfrac {2y} {2x} &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac {3 + 2y} {2x}\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: (5)/(3y) = a] and [MATH: (7)/(y) = b,] what is [MATH: a \u2212 b?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (16)/(3y)]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 (15)/(7y)]\nc. [MATH: (26)/(3y)]\nd. [MATH: (16)/(3y)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (2)/(3y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: (6y)/(5(1 \u2212 2y)) = p] and [MATH: (9 \u2212 4y)/(1 \u2212 2y) = q,] what is [MATH: p + q?]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (14y)/(5(1 \u2212 2y))]\nb. [MATH: (\u2212 14y + 45)/(5(1 \u2212 2y))]\nc. [MATH: (26y \u2212 45)/(5(1 \u2212 2y))]\nd. [MATH: (2y + 9)/(1 \u2212 2y)]\ne. [MATH: (45)/(1 \u2212 2y)]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Example: Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\dfrac{y}{x(1+y)} + \\dfrac{y}{1+y}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of [MATH: x(y+1)] if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by [MATH: x\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (x)/(x) \u22c5 (y)/(1 + y)|=; (y)/(x(1 + y)) + (xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y + xy)/(x(1 + y))|=; (y(1 + x))/(x(1 + y))]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (2)/(1 \u2212 z) \u2212 (1)/(w(1 \u2212 z)).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2w + 1)/(1 \u2212 z)]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(w(1 \u2212 z))]\nc. [MATH: (w \u2212 2)/(1 \u2212 z)]\nd. [MATH: (2w \u2212 1)/(w(1 \u2212 z))]\ne. [MATH: (w + 2)/(w(1 \u2212 z))]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (3)/(a(1 + b)) \u2212 (1)/(2a(1 + b)).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (5)/(2a(1 + b))]\nb. [MATH: (2)/(a(1 + b))]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (3)/(2a(1 + b))]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(a(1 + b))]\ne. [MATH: (5)/(a(1 + b))]"
  }
